roject in  her new york studio  i literally had to choose and  re-calibrate  every hole after the first hole because  each tree was in complete dialogue with  its neighbor  and spacing distancing  having mapped this out and studied it  made models do you still get surprised  i'm terrified it's not going to work  every single time  i have nightmares and i never know if  it's going to work  or not well how do you feel about it now  i'm feeling pretty good about it  i think we need to plant everything the  ohio-born  artist and architect has made a lasting  mark on the american landscape  designing the vietnam veterans memorial  in washington  the civil rights memorial in montgomery  alabama  and a series of wave field installations  like this one at the university of  michigan because i'm making them a  little closer and i want to see what  happens  but as lynn was in the final stages of  creating the ghost  forest in late january her husband of  nearly 25 years  daniel wolf died of a sudden heart  attack  you did this during a an especially  trying time  not just for the country but for  yourself  how did you get through it um i'm still  getting through it  yeah and my kids are getting through it  so it's it's you know it's it's very  um it's hard  somehow she went ahead with the project  did that help you in any way yeah  it did working is something that  creativity is a  to me it's a real gift maybe it's  because my dad was  in the arts my mother's a poet but  something that i really  have needed and loved  the 49 trees in the ghost forest will  stand  until the fall slowly graying as the  park  sees the seasons change they're majestic  trees they're beautiful just as a work  of art  they're 50 feet tall they're 8 60 to 80  years old  i'm hoping people are going to go  through and go these  trees are spiritually profound i mean  there's something  really there sometimes i think  haunting and beautiful can move you in a  way  that will hopefully get us to say this  